    "course_pol_en": "1. Diversity and Inclusion\nAll course activities will be carried out in an atmosphere of friendly participation and interaction among students, respecting and appreciating each student's personal experience, background and perspectives, such as various school systems at home and abroad, diverse enrollment and learning cultures, gender, etc. You are expected to apply the highest academic standards throughout this course and to treat fellow students with dignity and respect.\n2. Expected classroom behavior\nBehavior in the classroom should always be prompt, professional, prepared, and courteous. Effective learning can only be achieved by maintaining mutual dignity and mutual respect among classmates, teachers or other lecturers. In order to ensure a conducive learning environment, everyone is expected to do the following, abide by each other's classroom behavior rules, and get used to a culture of politeness.\n(1) Students should attend class on time. If you cannot arrive at the class on time due to your class time, please do not register for the class. Unless you are sick or there is an emergency, you should be in your classroom on time and in your seat before class starts. If you have to leave class early for any reason, please let me know in advance.\n(2) All students are expected to treat everyone with respect in the classroom and to behave reasonably, responsibly, and courteously at all times.\n(3) Do not talk, read, sleep or do other non-course-related homework during class, except for group discussions, otherwise marginal conversations should be minimized. If you are sitting next to someone who might be chatting with you during class, move to another seat so you don't give in to temptation.\n(4) You should listen attentively when classmates present or ask questions. When you present, you should speak loudly to your group or class so that they can hear you clearly.\n(5) Laptops or tablets are allowed to take notes in class, but they should be put away.\n(6) Make sure that mobile devices such as mobile phones are turned off during class, put away, and kept silent.",
    "course_rem": null,
    "course_rem_en": null,
    "ais_meno": null,
    "ais_meno_en": "<div>All exams are closed-book. Students are expected to work independently. The final report&nbsp;is required to introduce the life stories and achievements of Fourier and Laplace, and give your personal reflection report. The word count of your report is at least 1,000 words. Graded by: Life stories and achievements of Fourier and Laplace (60%), your personal reflection (40%).</div>\n",
    "ilearn_mk": "N"
  },
